# Knowledge nodes: name, short (<= 12 graphemes), anchor (calibration anchor C4 text).

node-s7-fnd-brayton_cycle =
    .name = Brayton cycle
    .short = Brayton
    .anchor = G0: before start
    .history = [Fact] George Brayton built the piston "Ready Motor" in 1872 [the patent and the name are confirmed only by a search snippet; the opened ASME page gives only the year 1872 for the cycle] with constant-pressure heat addition (a piston compressor and a piston expander). [Fact] The same scheme with a turbine as the expander was proposed by the Englishman John Barber in 1791. [Fact] The cycle is named after Brayton and underlies gas turbine calculations: compressor, combustor, turbine. [Assumption] In the game, knowledge "before 1945" is set by the rule RULE-G0, not by a publication date. [Not confirmed by a page] The specific work of Whittle and von Ohain is not shown on this cycle.

node-s8-fnd-boundary_layer =
    .name = Boundary layer
    .short = Bound. layer
    .anchor = Prandtl: boundary layer
    .history = [Fact] Ludwig Prandtl presented the boundary layer at the Third International Congress of Mathematicians in Heidelberg in 1904; the paper appeared in the congress proceedings in 1905 (pp. 484-491). [Fact] Original: "Über Flüssigkeitsbewegung bei sehr kleiner Reibung", Verhandlungen des III. Internationalen Mathematiker-Kongresses, Leipzig, Teubner, 1905, pp. 484-491. [Fact] The English translation "Motion of fluids with very little viscosity" (Dwight Miner) appeared as NACA TM-452 on 1 March 1928; the record is in NASA NTRS. [Assumption] The conclusion about explaining friction and flow separation follows from the content of the work; the page with the text was not opened.

node-s8-fnd-compressible_flow =
    .name = Compressible flow
    .short = Compr. flow
    .anchor = G0: before start
    .history = [Fact] Jakob Ackeret published a linearised theory of the supersonic airfoil (the first work in 1925 according to a university textbook; NASA gives 1928 as the start of the linear theory). [Fact] In 1908 Prandtl and his student Theodor Meyer extended shock wave theory to oblique shocks. [Fact] Adolf Busemann of Prandtl's Göttingen group described the history of the field in his 1971 review "Compressible Flow in the Thirties". [Not confirmed] The roles of Laval, Mach and the German institutes up to 1945 are not confirmed by the pages.

node-s5-fnd-precipitation_hardening =
    .name = Precipitation hardening
    .short = Hardening
    .anchor = Wilm: Al-Cu ageing (duralumin)
    .history = [Fact] Alfred Wilm discovered the ageing of aluminium alloys in the period 1903-1911; he patented the Al-Cu-Mg-Mn alloy in 1906 as duralumin. [Fact] The mechanism (Guinier-Preston zones) was found by small-angle X-ray scattering about 20 years after the discovery. [Not confirmed] The exact year of the explanation (1938) is not confirmed by a page; the discovery dates differ between sources (1901, 1903, 1906).

node-s8-fnd-transonic_aerodynamics =
    .name = Transonic aerodynamics
    .short = Transonic
    .anchor = Bell X-1: M 1.06, 14 Oct 1947
    .history = [Fact] Ray Wright of Langley proposed a slotted test section for the tunnel and Vernon Ward checked it on a small tunnel; the paper by Wright and Ward appeared in 1948. [Fact] According to NASA, the slotted section was first proven on the 16-foot tunnel in 1947, and in December 1950 the tunnel became the 16-Foot Transonic Tunnel. [Fact] Chuck Yeager exceeded the speed of sound in the X-1 in October 1947. [Fact] The competing annular tunnel (1947) was dismantled after the slotted one succeeded; the 1951 Collier Trophy went to John Stack and colleagues for this instrument.

node-s5-fnd-high_temperature_creep =
    .name = High-temperature creep
    .short = Hot creep
    .anchor = Nabarro: diffusional creep
    .history = [Fact] Nimonic 80 was created by Leonard Pfeil at the Inco-Mond works in Birmingham in 1941; it was used in the Power Jets W.2B. [Fact] Nimonic 80A (about 1945) went into the blades of the Rolls-Royce Nene and the de Havilland Ghost; then came Nimonic 90 (1945), 100 (1955), 105 (1960) and 115 (1964). [Not confirmed] The theory of creep as such (models of the 1950s-60s, Andrade in the 1910s) is not confirmed by the pages; all alloy dates come from a secondary copy of a reference article.

node-s7-fnd-turbine_heat_transfer =
    .name = Turbine heat transfer
    .short = Turb. heat
    .anchor = mid-window G3
    .history = [Fact] From 1945 NACA Lewis studied air flow in hollow blades; in 1948-1949 it developed finned blades and methods for predicting the performance of a cooled engine. [Fact] In 1950 experimental air-cooled turbines were tested on GE I-40 engines; water cooling (1950-1952) caused temperature gradients and blade failures and was abandoned. [Fact] In the 1960s-70s internal convective air cooling became the main method. [Assumption] Heat transfer theory (Nusselt numbers and others) is not shown separately by a page; the game date 1960 is conventional.

node-s5-fnd-solidification_science =
    .name = Solidification science
    .short = Solidif.
    .anchor = Rutter–Chalmers: constitutional supercooling
    .history = [Fact] The idea of constitutional supercooling was put forward qualitatively by Rutter and Chalmers to explain the cellular structure in tin, and the quantitative theory was given by Tiller, Jackson, Rutter and Chalmers in Acta Metallurgica 1:428-437 (1953). [Assumption] The link to directional and single-crystal casting of blades is not shown by these pages. The participation of Mullins in the original text is not confirmed.

node-s8-prn-gas_turbine =
    .name = Gas turbine
    .short = Gas turbine
    .anchor = bench-run turbojets of Whittle and von Ohain
    .history = [Fact] The He 178 (designer per the page: von Ohain) made the first turbojet flight on 27 August 1939. [Fact] The Gloster E.28/39 with Whittle's jet engine first flew on 15 May 1941 over Cranwell. [Not confirmed] The names HeS 3B and W.1, Rostock-Marienehe and the pilot Warsitz are not confirmed by an opened page. [Fact] Von Ohain developed his engine independently. [Not confirmed] Whittle's 1930 patent and the dead ends (Campini) are not confirmed by pages; the axial and centrifugal layouts were not compared.

node-s8-prn-swept_wing =
    .name = Swept wing
    .short = Swept wing
    .anchor = Busemann (Volta); Jones, NACA TN 1033 — 1945
    .history = [Fact] Adolf Busemann proposed the swept wing to reduce wave drag at the Volta Congress in Rome; the dates differ (NASA gives 1934, another source 1935). [Fact] Robert Jones (NACA Langley) independently reached the idea of sweep behind the Mach cone in 1945. [Fact] A Langley committee headed by Theodorsen recommended not publishing his report; later experiments (a 9-inch tunnel, Gilruth's free-fall models) confirmed the effect. [Not confirmed] The German projects Me 262 and P.1101 and the competing thin straight wing are not confirmed by pages.

node-s8-prn-afterburning =
    .name = Afterburning
    .short = Afterburning
    .anchor = first US afterburner (NACA AERL for the GE I-A)
    .history = [Fact] The first American afterburner circuit was built by AERL for the GE I-A in 1944; early tests gave only a nominal thrust gain because of poor combustion. [Fact] In 1946 a large afterburner was successfully run on the GE TG-180 in the Altitude Wind Tunnel. [Fact] Westinghouse tested the XJ34-WE-11 afterburner on an F-80A; a report of 20 January 1949 describes the first eight flights, operating up to 35,000 feet. [Fact] The first engine with an afterburner is named as the Jumo 004E (Germany). [Not confirmed] The British Nene/Avon and "30-50 %" are not confirmed by pages.

node-s5-prn-gamma_prime_alloy_design =
    .name = Gamma-prime alloy design
    .short = Gamma-prime
    .anchor = mid-window G2
    .history = [Fact] The gamma-prime phase Ni3(Al,Ti) is ordered, coherent with the matrix and is the main source of high-temperature strength; its strength is insensitive to temperature up to about 600 °C. [Fact] Turbine blades at more than 1000 °C contain a substantial fraction of this phase. [Fact] Nimonic 80 contains about 1.7 % Al and 20 % Cr, the rest nickel. [Not confirmed] The historical "deliberate composition design in the 1950s" and the cobalt dead ends are not confirmed by pages.

node-s8-prn-area_rule =
    .name = Area rule
    .short = Area rule
    .anchor = Whitcomb: area rule
    .history = [Fact] Richard Whitcomb (NACA Langley) formulated the area rule in late 1951; a confidential NACA memorandum appeared in September 1952 based on tunnel tests. [Fact] The idea: to minimise transonic drag, the cross-section area along the aircraft must vary like that of a smooth body. [Fact] Redesigning the F-102 by this rule gave, by NASA's estimate, about a 25 % gain in speed; Collier Trophy for 1954. [Assumption] The dead end "only thinning the wing" is not confirmed.

node-s8-prn-multi_spool_compression =
    .name = Multi-spool compression
    .short = Multi-spool
    .anchor = J57: first run of a two-spool turbojet (01.1950)
    .history = [Fact] In 1957 NACA Lewis (RM E57I27) studied the onset of surge in a two-spool turbojet in an altitude tunnel: rotating stall in the outer stage at low speeds leads to surge at medium speeds and requires bleeding air between the compressors. [Not confirmed by an opened page] The Pratt & Whitney J57 (JT3C) is considered the first two-spool turbojet; first run in January 1950. [Fact] The Rolls-Royce Olympus became the second two-spool axial turbojet, first run in May 1950. [Fact] P&W split the compressor into two parts on separate shafts with separate turbines; the layout removed surge, and the Olympus managed without variable guide vanes and bleed. [Assumption] The other side of the story (the GE J79 with variable stators) is not shown in these pages.

node-s8-prn-bypass_engine =
    .name = Bypass engine
    .short = Bypass
    .anchor = Conway RCo.2: first run (07.1952)
    .history = [Fact] Alan Griffith proposed bypass layouts as early as the 1930s. [Fact] The Rolls-Royce Conway (RCo.12) is named by the Smithsonian as the first production bypass engine: development was funded in 1952, first run in 1953, type test in 1955 with a record low specific fuel consumption for the time. [Fact] The Conway was fitted to the DC-8-40, Boeing 707-420, VC-10 and Victor; the bypass ratio was small (about 0.3 by one source). [Not confirmed] The dates of the JT3D and the transition to G4 are not confirmed by pages; Pratt & Whitney later outdid the Conway commercially.

node-s8-prn-film_cooling =
    .name = Film cooling
    .short = Film cooling
    .anchor = mid-window G3
    .history = [Fact] At NACA Lewis a review of film and transpiration cooling of blades was compiled by Eckert and Esgar (RM E50K15, about 1950). [Fact] A comparison of holes, grooves and slots (Eckert, Jackson, Francisco) showed that a continuous slot is better. [Fact] In the 1960s-70s internal convective air cooling became the main method. [Not confirmed] Serial use of film cooling in the 1960s and a gain of 100-200 K are not confirmed by pages.

node-s8-eng-turbojet_g1 =
    .name = Turbojet, gen 1
    .short = Turbojet 1
    .anchor = Meteor in service (07.1944)
    .history = Frank Whittle first ran his jet engine on a test stand on 12 April 1937, and the British Gloster E.28/39 with the Power Jets W.1 engine flew on 15 May 1941. In Germany Hans von Ohain built engines for Heinkel, and the He 178 with his engine became the first aircraft with a turbojet on 27 August 1939. The production Me 262 with two axial Jumo 004 engines entered service in 1944, but the Jumo 004B had a life of about 25 h before overhaul because of scarce materials. The British centrifugal layout and the German axial one competed. For the 1945 scenario this is the starting point: the generation is already in service. [Fact]

node-s8-eng-straight_wing_jet_airframe =
    .name = Straight-wing airframe
    .short = Str. wing
    .anchor = Meteor in service (07.1944)
    .history = The first Allied jet fighters had a straight wing: the Gloster Meteor first flew on 5 March 1943 and entered service on 27 July 1944 (No. 616 Squadron); the Lockheed XP-80 made its first flight on 8 January 1944 and began to reach units in late 1944-1945. Such machines were simpler and cheaper than swept ones, but near the speed of sound they suffered from rising drag. This pushed towards sweep after 1945. [Fact, dates from reference pages]

node-s8-eng-turbojet_g2 =
    .name = Turbojet, gen 2
    .short = Turbojet 2
    .anchor = Nene: first run 27.10.1944
    .history = The Rolls-Royce Nene was first run on 27 October 1944; its thrust was about 22 kN. The British government allowed deliveries to the USSR in September 1946 (10 engines) and March 1947 (15 more), after which Klimov copied it without a licence as the RD-45, then the VK-1 with a thrust of about 26.5 kN for the MiG-15. In the USA Pratt & Whitney built the Nene under licence as the J42 for the Grumman F9F Panther (first flight November 1947). Progress of this generation came from better materials and a larger centrifugal compressor. [Fact; details of the number of deliveries conflict in the sources]

node-s8-eng-swept_wing_airframe =
    .name = Swept-wing airframe
    .short = Swept frame
    .anchor = F-86: first flight 01.10.1947
    .history = Adolf Busemann presented the idea of the swept wing at the Volta conference in Rome in 1935. In 1945 Robert T. Jones of NACA independently obtained the theory of sweep (report of 21 June 1945). After the war the Americans used captured German data, and the North American F-86 Sabre with a sweep angle of 35° first flew on 1 October 1947. The Messerschmitt P.1101 was about 80 % complete at capture and provided the basis for the Bell X-5. Sweep led to longitudinal stability problems at low speeds, which were later solved along the way. [Fact]

node-s8-eng-afterburner =
    .name = Afterburner
    .short = Afterburner
    .anchor = mid-window G2
    .history = Afterburning was tried as early as 1941 on the motorjet Caproni Campini C.C.2 (a flight with reheat on 11 April 1941). In Britain test flights with reheat were made in late 1944 on a Gloster Meteor I with a Rolls-Royce W2/B23 engine, and ground tests on the Power Jets W2/700 in mid-1945. NACA published a theoretical work in January 1947. The thrust gain is large: the J57 has about 10,200 lbf dry and 16,000 with afterburner, roughly +57 % (calculated from reference data), at the price of a sharp rise in fuel consumption. The date of serial introduction (around 1950-51) is not confirmed by the sources. [Fact; the date of serial use [not confirmed]]

node-s8-eng-all_moving_tail =
    .name = All-moving tail
    .short = All-mov tail
    .anchor = X-1: all-moving stabiliser
    .history = The Bell X-1 had a horizontal stabiliser with adjustable incidence, recommended by John Stack and Robert Gilruth of NACA: near the speed of sound a shock wave deprived the elevator of effectiveness. On 14 October 1947 Chuck Yeager, who at Mach 0.94 ran out of elevator travel, controlled pitch by moving the stabiliser and broke the sound barrier. A strictly "all-moving" tail (stabiliser and elevator in one element) appeared later: the B-47 (1951) is named as the first dedicated jet bomber with a one-piece stabiliser, and on the F-86 it was added after the first production batches. [Fact; the year for the F-86 is not given in the source]

node-s8-eng-turbojet_g3 =
    .name = Turbojet, gen 3
    .short = Turbojet 3
    .anchor = J57 (two-spool, 10,000 lbf)
    .history = The Pratt & Whitney J57 was first run in 1950; it was the first US engine of the 10,000 lbf class, with a thrust of about 11,700 lbf dry and a pressure ratio of 11.5-12.5. It was fitted to the B-52, F-100 and F-102. The General Electric J79 (first run on 8 June 1954) had variable compressor guide vanes and a pressure ratio of about 13.5, and was used on the F-104 and F-4. The Rolls-Royce Avon, the company's first axial engine, was run on 25 March 1947 (12 compressor stages). [Fact]

node-s8-eng-area_ruled_airframe =
    .name = Area-ruled airframe
    .short = Area-ruled
    .anchor = YF-102A: first flight 20.12.1954
    .history = Richard Whitcomb (NACA Langley) understood the area rule in 1951, and the NACA memorandum appeared in September 1952. The Convair YF-102 first flew on 23-24 October 1953 and could not go supersonic (about Mach 0.98); the redesigned fuselage allowed it to exceed the speed of sound in 1954, and the production F-102A flew from 1955. Whitcomb received the Collier Trophy for 1954. Otto Frenzl independently found the rule at Junkers in 1943-45, but it was not applied. The redesign delayed the aircraft's service by about two years. [Fact]

node-s8-eng-delta_wing_airframe =
    .name = Delta-wing airframe
    .short = Delta wing
    .anchor = YF-102A (delta)
    .history = Alexander Lippisch developed delta shapes in Germany in the 1940s and worked in the USA after the war. The first American prototype was the Convair XF-92A (first flight 1948). The YF-102 had a delta wing, and the Dassault Mirage III with a delta first flew on 17 November 1956; its Mirage IIIA reached Mach 2.2 in 1958. Advantages: stiffness and volume for fuel; disadvantages: drag in manoeuvre and a high landing speed. [Fact]

node-s8-eng-variable_geometry_inlet =
    .name = Variable-geometry inlet
    .short = Var. inlet
    .anchor = mid-window G3
    .history = The McDonnell XF4H-1 (the future F-4 Phantom II, first flight 27 May 1958) had an inlet with one fixed and one movable ramp, designed for pressure between Mach 1.4 and 2.2. The Mirage III had movable half-cones ("souris") that moved forward as the Mach number rose. On the MiG-21 the cone had three positions, and on the MiG-21PF the cone position changed continuously. On Concorde the movable panels engage above Mach 1.3 and give about 63 % of thrust at cruise. [Fact; the F-4 source is internally inconsistent]

node-s8-eng-air_cooled_turbine_blade =
    .name = Air-cooled turbine blade
    .short = Air-cooled
    .anchor = mid-window G4 (Conway — first with cooled blades; date not found)
    .history = NASA Glenn describes turbine cooling as a priority of the late 1940s and the 1950s: in the Special Projects Laboratory cooled blades and cooling systems were built. In the early 1970s a US Air Force programme funded a blade with film and convective cooling. Which engine first received serial cooled blades is not named by the sources. The game date 1970 is set by the rule RULE-MID. [Fact in part; the time of serial introduction [not confirmed]]

node-s8-eng-turbofan_g4 =
    .name = Turbofan, gen 4
    .short = Turbofan 4
    .anchor = TF30 (afterburning turbofan): first flight
    .history = The Rolls-Royce Conway became the first bypass turbojet in service: the prototype RCo.2 was first run in July 1952 (10,000 lbf), with a bypass ratio of about 0.3. The Pratt & Whitney TF30 is named as the first production bypass engine with an afterburner; it first flew in 1964, and the TF30-P-1 gave 8,500 lbf dry and 18,500 with afterburner. Bypass reduced fuel consumption through the air going around the core. [Fact]

node-s8-eng-mach2_airframe_g4 =
    .name = Mach-2 airframe, gen 4
    .short = Mach-2 frame
    .anchor = F-15: first flight 27.07.1972
    .history = The first aircraft to exceed Mach 2 was the Douglas D-558-2 Skyrocket on 20 November 1953 with NACA pilot Scott Crossfield (Mach 2.005). The Lockheed XF-104 first flew on 4 March 1954, and the production F-104A entered service in January-February 1958 as the first production Mach 2 aircraft. The Mirage IIIA reached Mach 2.2 on 24 October 1958, and the MiG-21 entered service in 1959. The increase in speed required thin wings and new materials. The game date 1972 does not match the real dates. [Fact]

node-s5-cmp-heat_resistant_alloy =
    .name = Heat-resistant alloy
    .short = Hot alloy
    .anchor = Nimonic 80
    .history = The nickel superalloys of the Nimonic series were made at Wiggin (a division of Inco-Mond); Nimonic 80 appeared in 1941, and the Power Jets W.2B used alloy 80. Germany, short of nickel, cobalt and molybdenum, used aluminised mild steel and hollow Cromadur blades with 12 % chromium for the hot parts of the Jumo 004, which is why the 004B life was about 25 h. Development went from Nimonic 80 (1941) to 80A and 90 (1945), 100 (1955) and 105 (1960). [Fact]

node-s5-cmp-aluminium_alloy_sheet =
    .name = Aluminium alloy sheet
    .short = Al sheet
    .anchor = duralumin: licence of Dürener Metallwerke
    .history = Alfred Wilm discovered the ageing of an aluminium-copper alloy around 1901 and by 1906 had brought the composition to a practical one; German patent DE 244554 of 20 March 1909. The alloy came to be called duralumin after the patent was bought and the material put on the market. By the 1930s it became the main aircraft material. The name of the manufacturing company is not confirmed in the checked sources. [Fact; the exact year of the discovery differs between sources]

node-s5-cmp-titanium_alloy =
    .name = Titanium alloy
    .short = Ti alloy
    .anchor = Ti-6Al-4V
    .history = Wilhelm Kroll began experiments on producing titanium in 1930 and by 1938 had obtained about 50 pounds of metal; then the US Bureau of Mines developed his process. The US Army funded the Ti-6Al-4V alloy. The Douglas X-3 Stiletto (first flight 20 October 1952) was the first to use titanium in large airframe elements. The Lockheed A-12 (first flight 25 April 1962) was 93 % titanium by structural mass, and the SR-71 about 85 %. The year Ti-6Al-4V appeared is not confirmed. [Fact; the year of the alloy [not confirmed]]

node-s8-cmp-turbine_blade_forged =
    .name = Forged turbine blade
    .short = Forged blade
    .anchor = Nimonic 80 — standard rotor-blade alloy of British gas turbines
    .history = In first-generation British engines the turbine blades were made of Nimonic nickel alloys, more heat-resistant than ordinary steel. Germany, short of raw materials, used hollow blades of Cromadur alloy (12 % chromium) in the Jumo 004. The method of making them (forging and machining) is not described separately in the checked sources. [Fact in part; the manufacturing technology [not confirmed]]

node-s8-cmp-turbine_blade_cast =
    .name = Cast turbine blade
    .short = Cast blade
    .anchor = mid-window G3
    .history = Investment casting became the main way of making gas turbine blades; directional solidification for columnar and single-crystal structures was developed in the 1960s-70s. The exact year cast blades entered a production engine is not given in the checked sources; the game date 1960 is set by the rule RULE-MID. [Fact in part; the date [not confirmed]]

node-s8-cmp-turbine_blade_cooled =
    .name = Cooled blade (component)
    .short = Cooled blade
    .anchor = mid-window G4
    .history = Blades with internal channels and film cooling were used in engines from the 1960s-70s: in the early 1970s a US Air Force programme funded a blade with film and convective cooling. NASA Glenn links the development of cooling to the priorities of the 1940s-50s. The date 1970 is set by the rule RULE-MID. [Fact in part; the date of the first serial use [not confirmed]]

node-s8-cmp-axial_compressor_assembly =
    .name = Axial compressor assembly
    .short = Axial comp.
    .anchor = mid-window G2
    .history = The axial compressor was used in the Metrovick F.2 (first run November 1941, 9 stages; first flight on a Lancaster on 29 June 1943), the BMW 003 (first run August 1940, 7 stages) and the Jumo 004 (8 stages). The Rolls-Royce Avon was the company's first axial engine (run on 25 March 1947, 12 stages). The axial layout gave a larger flow at a smaller diameter than the centrifugal one of Whittle and the Nene, but was harder to manufacture. [Fact]

node-s8-cmp-afterburner_unit =
    .name = Afterburner unit
    .short = AB unit
    .anchor = mid-window G2
    .history = The afterburner as an assembly: fuel nozzles, flame holders and an adjustable nozzle behind the turbine. The first flight tests of reheat in Britain were made in late 1944 on a Gloster Meteor I with a Rolls-Royce W2/B23 engine. On the J57 thrust rose from 10,200 to 16,000 lbf with afterburner. The year serial production began is not given in the sources; the date 1951 is set by the rule RULE-MID. [Fact in part; the date [not confirmed]]

node-s5-prc-light_alloy_rolling =
    .name = Light alloy rolling
    .short = Al rolling
    .anchor = duralumin in production
    .history = [Fact] Alfred Wilm (Germany) found that a quenched Al alloy with 4 % Cu hardens at room temperature within a few days (1903 per Wikipedia; another source gives a different date, the dates are disputed). Duralumin was introduced in 1909; Wilm's patents were bought by the firm Dürener Metallwerke, which put the material on the market. Sheet duralumin was used in the frames of rigid airships; Hugo Junkers tried it in the J 3 aircraft in 1916, but the work was stopped before the machine was completed. [Not confirmed] The year of the first airship with duralumin and the year the works rolled the sheet: the pages do not give them.

node-s5-prc-nicr_alloy_melt =
    .name = NiCr alloy melting
    .short = NiCr melt
    .anchor = Nimonic 80 in production
    .history = [Fact] The Nimonic nickel-chromium alloys were developed by the British Mond Nickel (Wiggin, Birmingham, later Inco, now a trademark of Special Metals). Nimonic 80 was introduced around 1941, 80A in 1944-45, Nimonic 90 in 1945; by one weak source Nimonic 75 appeared in 1939. An alloy with a gamma-prime phase from Ti and Al was used for turbine blades, and Nimonic 80 was used in the Power Jets W.2B. Chromium-nickel steels and austenitic stainless steels (1910-15) were an earlier, less heat-resistant approach. Corrected: the earlier text about Nimonic 75 in 1941 and about the W.1/Welland was not confirmed.

node-s5-prc-superalloy_melt_g2 =
    .name = Superalloy melting
    .short = Superalloy
    .anchor = mid-window G2
    .history = [Fact] By 1929 an 80Ni-20Cr alloy with small additions of Ti and Al (the first gamma-prime precipitates) had become the norm, and in the 1940s Nimonic alloys with the gamma-prime phase were used for blades. Around 1950 vacuum melting was commercialised, and in the 1950s vacuum casting of blades appeared. For the hot section this is a step from "Nimonic" to the generation of cast superalloys. [Not confirmed] The dates of Waspaloy (Pratt & Whitney) and Inconel X-750: no opened page gave them.

node-s5-prc-vacuum_induction_melt =
    .name = Vacuum induction melting
    .short = Vac. melt
    .anchor = industrial VIM of superalloys (early 1950s)
    .history = [Fact] Vacuum induction melting was developed by Heraeus Vacuumschmelze and Wilhelm Rohn in Hanau around 1917, with a German patent application filed in January 1918 (the first American prototype of 1920 by Northrup: no source reference). At first it was used for cobalt and nickel; for superalloys vacuum melting became commercial around 1950. It removes hydrogen and nitrogen and allows Al and Ti to be held precisely. The earlier text about the firm Cannon-Muskegon is not confirmed and was removed.

node-s5-prc-titanium_reduction_forging =
    .name = Titanium reduction and forging
    .short = Ti forging
    .anchor = DuPont: first sale of titanium sponge (09.1948)
    .history = [Fact] Wilhelm Kroll reported the magnesiothermic reduction of TiCl4 to the Electrochemical Society; his paper "The Production of Ductile Titanium" is dated 1940; later in the USA he worked on zirconium at the Albany Research Center. The Hunter (sodium) process ceased to be commercial in the 1990s. In aviation, titanium gave a gain in mass and heat resistance. [Not confirmed] The 1940 patent, the first industrial production by DuPont in 1948 and the role of the US Bureau of Mines: the opened page does not contain them, a source is needed (for example DOE/ARC-1999-060).

node-s5-prc-ds_casting =
    .name = Directional solidification casting
    .short = DS casting
    .anchor = DS blades: P&W patent 1966; first use on the J58
    .history = [Fact] Blades with directional solidification (columnar grains) raise resistance to creep and thermal fatigue. They first appeared in series on the J58 engines of the SR-71; single-crystal research began in the 1960s at Pratt & Whitney and reached service about a decade later. Barry Piearcey found that bending the mould at a right angle reduces the number of columnar crystals, and Tony Giamei (P&W) used this for the single crystal. [Not confirmed] "1969" as the year of serial introduction of DS and the name Versnyder: the pages do not give them.

node-s5-prc-alloying_element_refining =
    .name = Alloying element refining
    .short = Alloy refin.
    .anchor = G0: before start
    .history = [Fact] Aluminothermy (Hans Goldschmidt) was discovered in 1893 and patented in 1895 (granted in 1898); with it metallic chromium is obtained from Cr2O3 and some ores are purified. This method gave chromium and manganese for alloys; superalloys also need Co, Mo, W, Nb, Ti and Al. [Not confirmed] That by 1945 the purification of these elements had reached factory level in the USA: the page does not say; the entry remains a generalisation.

node-s5-prc-high_purity_nickel_refining =
    .name = High-purity nickel refining
    .short = Pure Ni
    .anchor = window G1
    .history = [Fact] Ludwig Mond's carbonyl process (1890) purifies nickel: carbon monoxide forms Ni(CO)4, which decomposes to nickel of very high purity in one pass. The method was used by the International Nickel Company (Sudbury basin). The toxicity of the carbonyl makes it hard for mass production. [Not confirmed] The plant at Clydach and the Hybinette electrolytic method: the opened pages do not name them.

node-s5-prc-ceramic_shell_moulding =
    .name = Ceramic shell moulding
    .short = Ceramic mold
    .anchor = mid-window G3
    .history = [Fact] Investment casting in dental practice was described by F. Philbrook in 1897, and W. Taggart (Chicago) developed the method in 1907; the Second World War increased demand for precise parts from hard-to-machine alloys. The cobalt alloy Vitallium was created by Albert Merrick for Austenal in 1932; during the war the alloy Haynes Stellite 21 was proposed for turbochargers, after precision casting had been shown on dentures. The ceramic shell is built up in layers of slurry on a wax pattern. [Not confirmed] Who introduced the shell and when, and the date 1960: the pages do not give them.

node-s6-prc-precision_gear_bearing =
    .name = Precision gears and bearings
    .short = Gears, bear.
    .anchor = G0: before start
    .history = [Assumption] Gears are finished by grinding after cutting; precise gears and bearings are needed for reduction gearboxes and turbine shaft supports. [Not confirmed] The inventor of gear grinding (Max Maag, 1913) and the link to the Rolls-Royce Dart: the opened page on grinding (Wikipedia) does not name them and refers to R. Woodbury's book "History of the Gear-Cutting Machine" (1958). The history of this node remains weak.

node-s6-prc-centrifugal_impeller_machining =
    .name = Centrifugal impeller machining
    .short = Impeller
    .anchor = window G1
    .history = [Fact] Frank Whittle's Power Jets W.1 engine was first run on 14 December 1940, and the Gloster E.28/39 first flew on 15 May 1941 (Cranwell). The compressor was a single-stage centrifugal one with a double-sided impeller of Hiduminium RR.59 alloy. The Rolls-Royce Derwent went into series in 1944 and became the Meteor's engine. [Not confirmed] The years of the Welland and the Goblin: the opened pages do not name them.

node-s6-prc-precision_blade_forging =
    .name = Precision blade forging
    .short = Blade forge
    .anchor = forged Nimonic 80 blades
    .history = [Fact] The turbine blades of the Junkers Jumo 004 were hollow: they were bent and welded from Cromadur sheet (Krupp, about 70 % Fe, 18 % Mn, 12 % Cr) and cooled by compressor air. The life of the early 004B engines was about 10-25 h, with overhaul every 30-50 h; series production began only in early 1944, and 5-8 thousand engines were built. Anselm Franz headed the team in 1939 and chose the axial compressor. The British went another way: blades of Nimonic.

node-s6-prc-axial_compressor_machining =
    .name = Axial compressor machining
    .short = Axial mach.
    .anchor = mid-window G2
    .history = [Fact] The Pratt & Whitney J57 was first run in January 1950; it is a two-spool engine (two compressors, two turbines). The pressure ratio of the military J57-P-23 is 11.5:1 and of the civil JT3C-7 12.5:1; the thrust of the J57-P-23 rises from 11,700 to 17,200 lbf with afterburner. The earlier "about 12:1" was corrected. The Jumo 004 axial compressor (Franz) was in series from 1944. [Not confirmed] The dates of the General Electric J35 and Allison: not checked.

node-s6-prc-investment_cast_blades =
    .name = Investment-cast blades
    .short = Inv. casting
    .anchor = mid-window G3
    .history = [Fact] Most turbine blades are cast by the lost-wax method; hollow blades are made with a ceramic core that is dissolved, leaving the cooling channels. Early blades were of Nimonic in Whittle's engines, and vacuum induction melting in the 1950s raised the temperature limit. The cobalt Vitallium (1932) was used in turbochargers. [Not confirmed] "Around 1960" as the year of the transition to cast blades in production jet engines.

node-s6-prc-cooled_blade_drilling =
    .name = Cooled blade drilling
    .short = Blade drill
    .anchor = mid-window G4
    .history = [Fact] In 1943 B. R. and N. I. Lazarenko were given the task of protecting tungsten contacts from erosion and found that in a liquid dielectric erosion can be controlled: this is how the spark erosion machine appeared. Today it drills rows of holes on the leading and trailing edges of blades; gas through the holes lets them work at a higher temperature. [Not confirmed] The time film cooling and laser drilling appeared (around 1970): the opened pages do not contain it.

node-s8-prc-stressed_skin_assembly =
    .name = Stressed-skin assembly
    .short = Skin assy
    .anchor = G0: before start
    .history = [Fact] The all-metal Junkers J 1 (1915) had a metal skin on a frame (semi-monocoque, not a true monocoque). The Dornier-Zeppelin Lindau D.I (1918) became the first all-metal monocoque in series; aluminium monocoques spread only in the mid-1930s. The behaviour of thin-walled beams in tension fields is described in NACA TM-604 (part I: general theories). [Not confirmed] The author Herbert Wagner, the year 1929/1931 and the Boeing 247 (1933): the pages do not name them.

node-s8-prc-jet_engine_assembly_test =
    .name = Jet engine assembly and test
    .short = Engine assy
    .anchor = production turbojets for the Meteor
    .history = [Fact] The Me 262 with a jet engine first flew on 18 July 1942 (Fritz Wendel, Leipheim); the test unit Erprobungskommando 262 was formed on 19 April 1944. The Jumo 004A engine reached an overhaul interval of 50 h by mid-1943, the 004B required a turbine check after 25 h, with a wheel limit of 35 h. The first combat sortie of the Me 262 was made on 25 July 1944. A flight of the Me 262 with a piston engine had taken place as early as 18 April 1941.

node-s8-prc-swept_wing_fabrication =
    .name = Swept wing fabrication
    .short = Swept fab.
    .anchor = F-86 (first flight)
    .history = [Fact] Adolf Busemann presented the principle of independence as applied to sweep at the Volta Congress in Rome in 1935. Robert T. Jones (NACA) came up with the swept wing in 1944, his report on the thin wing is dated May 1945, and the analysis appeared in open print in 1946; Jones worked independently. The XP-86 prototype first flew on 1 October 1947. Manufacture requires precise joints of spars and skin. [Not confirmed] The dates of the first flights of the B-47 and the MiG-15: the opened pages did not give them.

node-s8-prc-machined_integral_skins =
    .name = Machined integral skins
    .short = Int. skins
    .anchor = mid-window G3
    .history = [Fact] The birth of numerical control is usually linked to John Parsons and Frank Stulen (Parsons Corp., Traverse City, Michigan); the first CNC machines were built in the 1940s-50s, on ordinary machine tools with motors and punched tape. They received the National Medal of Technology in 1985. Integrally machined panels became practical with CNC. [Not confirmed] The MIT machine of 1952, the participation of the Air Force and specific aircraft: the opened page does not contain them, and the MIT page returned 403.

node-s8-prc-afterburner_fabrication =
    .name = Afterburner fabrication
    .short = AB fabric.
    .anchor = mid-window G2
    .history = [Fact] Flight tests of the Rolls-Royce W2/B23 afterburner on a Gloster Meteor I were made in late 1944, and ground tests of the Power Jets W2/700 in mid-1945. The Caproni Campini C.C.2 aircraft flew with its afterburner working in April 1941. American work of 1948 led to installation on the Pirate, Starfire and Scorpion. The J57-P-23 with afterburner gives up to 17,200 lbf. [Not confirmed] The earlier statement about the first series on the F-86D around 1949.

node-s8-app-jet_fighter_g1 =
    .name = Jet fighter, gen 1
    .short = Fighter 1
    .anchor = Meteor in service (07.1944)
    .history = [Fact] The Gloster E.28/39 with Whittle's engine first flew on 15 May 1941, and the prototype Meteor DG206 (Michael Daunt) flew on 5 March 1943. The first Meteors reached No. 616 Squadron in July 1944 (Culmhead, 12 July), and the first combat sortie against V-1s took place on 27 July 1944; the speed of the F.1 was about 671 km/h. The Me 262 first flew on jet power on 18 July 1942, with the Erprobungskommando unit from April 1944 and its first combat on 25 July 1944. The earlier "800-870 km/h for all" was corrected. [Not confirmed] The He 280 and the exact speed of the Me 262.

node-s8-app-jet_fighter_g2 =
    .name = Jet fighter, gen 2
    .short = Fighter 2
    .anchor = F-86 (first flight 01.10.1947)
    .history = [Fact] The XP-86 prototype first flew on 1 October 1947 (George Welch, Muroc). The F-86 was introduced into the US Air Force in 1949 (94th Squadron, 1st Fighter Wing) and was the main fighter in Korea in 1950-53, where it met the swept MiG-15; the initial tallies of 792:78 were later revised. The speed record of the F-86A: 671 mph (1,080 km/h) on 15 September 1948. [Not confirmed] The dates of the MiG-15 and F-84: the pages were not checked.

node-s8-app-jet_fighter_g3 =
    .name = Jet fighter, gen 3
    .short = Fighter 3
    .anchor = F-100: first flight 25.05.1953
    .history = [Fact] The F-100 Super Sabre first flew in 1953 (YF-100A, 25 May 1953, George Welch) and was the first production US Air Force aircraft to reach supersonic speed in level flight; it entered service on 27 September 1954 (479th Wing), after which operations were suspended after accidents until February 1955. The F-100D flew on 24 January 1956 (speed 1,437 km/h at the NASM museum). [Not confirmed] The dates of the F-102, F-104, MiG-19, Mirage III and "up to Mach 2": the pages were not checked; the earlier years are left as [Assumption].

node-s8-app-jet_fighter_g4 =
    .name = Jet fighter, gen 4
    .short = Fighter 4
    .anchor = F-15: first flight 27.07.1972
    .history = [Fact] The F-15 Eagle first flew on 27 July 1972 and was adopted on 9 January 1976 (555th Fighter Squadron), with two Pratt & Whitney F100 engines with afterburner and a Hughes APG-63 pulse-Doppler radar with look-down capability. The radars were later upgraded to AESA. [Not confirmed] The dates of the F-14, F-16, MiG-29, Su-27 and the F-16 fly-by-wire: not checked.

node-s8-fnd-electronic_flight_control_theory =
    .name = Electronic flight control theory
    .short = E-control
    .anchor = F-8 DFBW: first digital fly-by-wire flight (25.05.1972)
    .history = [Fact] The F-8C DFBW (NASA 802) made its first flight on 25 May 1972, pilot Gary Krier. [Fact] The system used the Apollo command module computer (about 38 thousand words of memory, of which 36 thousand permanent). [Fact] An analogue set of three computers served as backup. [Fact] According to NASA, the project confirmed the principles of fly-by-wire for military and civil aircraft, and the side stick went to the F-16. [Not confirmed] The theoretical part (Nyquist, Kalman) and the analogue Avro Arrow and Concorde are not confirmed by pages.

node-s8-fnd-edge_wave_diffraction =
    .name = Edge-wave diffraction theory
    .short = Edge waves
    .anchor = Ufimtsev: method of edge waves (1962, estimate)
    .history = [Fact] Pyotr Ufimtsev published "The Method of Edge Waves in the Physical Theory of Diffraction" at Sovetskoe Radio (Moscow, 1962). [Fact] Joseph Keller gave the geometrical theory of diffraction (JOSA 52:116-130, 1962). [Fact] An English translation of Ufimtsev's book was issued by the US Air Force Foreign Technology Division in 1971 (FTD-HC-23-259-71). [Fact] The physical theory corrects physical optics by means of edge currents. [Assumption] The application to the calculation of low-observable shapes is confirmed through Echo 1 (the node low_observable_shaping).

node-s5-prn-single_crystal_growth =
    .name = Single-crystal growth
    .short = Mono-crystal
    .anchor = estimate 1974
    .history = [Fact] According to an ASME brochure, the JT9D-7R4 (Boeing 747, DC-10, A300) around 1980 became the first commercial application of single-crystal turbine parts. [Fact] The alloy PWA 1480 is described by NASA as commercial; it was chosen for the blades of the high-pressure turbopump of the Space Shuttle main engine. [Not confirmed] That PWA 1480 was exactly what was fitted to the JT9D, and the temperature gain of 30-50 K, are not confirmed by pages; earlier directional castings are mentioned without a source.

node-s5-prn-fibre_composite_laminate_design =
    .name = Fibre composite laminate design
    .short = Composites
    .anchor = F-14: boron-epoxy surfaces (1970)
    .history = [Fact] The first serial large military part made of boron fibre was the horizontal stabilisers of the F-14 (boron epoxy, Avco and Hamilton Standard); this was not carbon fibre. [Fact] RAE Farnborough began developing high-modulus PAN-based carbon fibre in 1963; the process was patented by the Ministry of Defence, with licences passed through NRDC to Rolls-Royce, Morganite and Courtaulds. [Fact] An early problem: weak interlaminar shear. [Fact] The wing of the Fairey Delta E10/47 became the first flown primary composite structure (1947), and a Spitfire fuselage of Aerolite was made in 1940. [Not confirmed] The classical laminate theory of the "late 1960s" is not confirmed by pages.

node-s8-prn-low_observable_shaping =
    .name = Low-observable shaping
    .short = LO shaping
    .anchor = estimate 1975
    .history = [Fact] The first flight of Have Blue (HB1001) took place on 1 December 1977, pilot Bill Park, Groom Lake. [Fact] The F-117A became combat-ready in October 1983. [Fact] The calculation of the reflected signal was done by Denys Overholser with the program Echo 1; Ufimtsev's edge diffraction theory (translation of 1971) was inserted into it. [Fact] The program calculated in only two dimensions, which is why the shape came out faceted. [Fact] Echo 1 is dated 1975 (Air & Space Forces). [Not confirmed] Only the comparison with the radar-absorbing coatings of the SR-71 is not confirmed by pages.

node-s8-eng-fly_by_wire_relaxed_stability =
    .name = Fly-by-wire, relaxed stability
    .short = Fly-by-wire
    .anchor = YF-16: first flight (02.02.1974)
    .history = The NASA F-8 Digital Fly-By-Wire made the first fully digital flight on 25 May 1972 (pilot Gary Krier) on an Apollo Guidance Computer, at first with an analogue backup system. The General Dynamics YF-16 first flew on 2 February 1974 (an accidental hop during taxiing on 20 January); it has a quadruple-redundant fly-by-wire system and deliberately reduced static stability, and the F-16 entered service in 1978. Concorde had analogue fly-by-wire, and the Airbus A320 entered service in 1988. [Fact]

node-s8-eng-turbofan_g5 =
    .name = Turbofan, gen 5
    .short = Turbofan 5
    .anchor = F100 DEEC: digital engine control (1981-83)
    .history = The Pratt & Whitney F100-PW-100 first flew on the F-15 on 27 July 1972, with an afterburning thrust of 23,930 lbf and a thrust-to-weight ratio of 7.88. The PW-220 model received a digital electronic engine control (DEEC). The F100 is used on the F-15 and F-16, and by the company's data its flying time has exceeded 30 million hours. The game date 1981 is set by the G56 scenario. [Fact]

node-s8-eng-composite_airframe =
    .name = Composite airframe
    .short = Comp. airf.
    .anchor = YAV-8B: first flight (09.11.1978)
    .history = The McDonnell Douglas AV-8B Harrier II has about 26 % composites by airframe mass and saves about 217 kg (480 lb) compared with a metal structure; the prototype YAV-8B flew on 9 November 1978, and the production AV-8B on 5 November 1981. The B-2 Spirit (first flight 17 July 1989) consists mainly of carbon-graphite composite. The first use of composites on the F-14 could not be verified. [Fact; early applications [not confirmed]]

node-s8-eng-low_observable_airframe =
    .name = Low-observable airframe
    .short = LO airframe
    .anchor = Have Blue: first flight (01.12.1977)
    .history = Lockheed Have Blue went into flight testing in April 1977, and the first flight of the F-117 took place in June 1981. The shape was calculated with Denys Overholser's program Echo 1, based on Pyotr Ufimtsev's work of the 1960s; the US Air Force translated his paper in 1971. The B-2 first flew on 17 July 1989. Low observability changed aviation tactics: the machine is invisible to radar at the ranges that matter. [Fact]

node-s8-eng-thrust_vectoring_supercruise_engine =
    .name = Thrust-vectoring supercruise engine
    .short = TVC engine
    .anchor = YF-22: thrust vectoring 15.11.1990, supercruise 03.11.1990
    .history = The Rockwell-MBB X-31 first flew on 11 October 1990 with three deflector vanes on the nozzle of a GE F404 engine, and tests at NASA Dryden ran from 1992. The Pratt & Whitney F119 passed ground tests of the EMD version from February 1993 and first flew on the F-22 on 7 September 1997; the flat nozzle deflects in pitch by ±20°. The F-22 holds supersonic speed without afterburner at Mach 1.5-1.8. The ATF request was issued in May 1983. [Fact]

node-s8-cmp-turbine_blade_single_crystal =
    .name = Single-crystal turbine blade
    .short = SC blade
    .anchor = estimate 1982
    .history = Research on single-crystal blades began in the 1960s at Pratt & Whitney and took about ten years to reach service. The directional solidification method was developed in the 1960s-70s; first-generation alloys (PWA 1480) date from the 1980s, and the second and third generations contain about 3 and 6 % rhenium. I could not confirm the first production engine (around 1982, JT9D-7R4). A gain in operating temperature of the order of tens of degrees is comparable to replacing DS blades. [Fact in part; the first serial use and specific figures [not confirmed]]

node-s5-prc-single_crystal_casting =
    .name = Single-crystal casting
    .short = SC casting
    .anchor = estimate 1982
    .history = [Fact] Single-crystal blades (no grain boundaries) were developed at Pratt & Whitney from the 1960s and reached service about a decade later; Barry Piearcey proposed bending the mould to select a single grain, and Tony Giamei (P&W) started the single crystal. The first generation of single-crystal superalloys (PWA 1480, René N4, SRR99) belongs to the 1980s. [Not confirmed] The JT9D-7R4 engine and 1982: the opened pages do not name them.

node-s8-prc-composite_layup_cure =
    .name = Composite layup and cure
    .short = Layup+cure
    .anchor = estimate 1978
    .history = [Fact] The NASA ACEE (Aircraft Energy Efficiency) programme and composite research at Langley ran in 1975-1986 (the title of an NTRS report). [Assumption] Composite tail surfaces and secondary structures appeared on the F-14 (1970), F-15, F/A-18, AV-8B and Boeing 757/767, but none of the opened pages confirmed this, and the link to the NASA report did not open because of a timeout. [Not confirmed] The shares of composites, the Rolls-Royce Hyfil dead end.

node-s5-prc-low_observable_finishing =
    .name = Low-observable finishing
    .short = LO finish
    .anchor = estimate 1981
    .history = [Fact] Lockheed built two Have Blue demonstrators at its own expense; the first flight was on 1 December 1977 (Bill Park), with 88 flights until 1979. The first F-117 flew on 18 June 1981 (Hal Farley), initial operational capability in October 1983, publicly acknowledged on 10 November 1988, retired in April 2008. About a tonne of radar-absorbing material in sheets was glued on and the seams were closed with filler. The concept of facets was justified by Denys Overholser's team. [Not confirmed] Ufimtsev's work of 1962 (found in search but the pages were not opened) and the composition of the coating.

node-s8-app-jet_fighter_g5 =
    .name = Jet fighter 4+ (fly-by-wire)
    .short = Fighter 4+
    .anchor = Mirage 2000 in service (1984)
    .history = [Fact] The modern definition of the fifth generation: low observability, a low-probability-of-intercept radar, a manoeuvrable layout, supersonic cruise without afterburner, and sensor data fusion. Aircraft in service as of December 2025: the F-22 (US Air Force, 2005), F-35 (Marine Corps, 2015), J-20 (2017), Su-57 (2020), J-35 (2025). The F-117A is a strike aircraft, not a fighter, and is usually not included in this generation. The game year 1984 corresponds to demonstrators and the start of the ATF programme, not to entry into service (see the note below). In the game the node is labelled "4+ (fly-by-wire)": by the generally accepted definition (low observability, supersonic cruise without afterburner) the first fifth generation in service was the F-22 (2005); the node's years are entry into service by the leader in our history, in the game it may be different.

node-s8-app-jet_fighter_g6 =
    .name = Jet fighter, low-observable, F-22 class
    .short = Stealth F-22
    .anchor = F-22: initial operational capability (12.2005)
    .history = [Fact] The sixth generation does not yet have an accepted definition: higher low observability, range, beyond-visual-range weapons, possibly command of unmanned wingmen. The US Air Force NGAD was started in 2014, the Boeing F-47 was selected in March 2025, with entry into service expected in the 2030s; GCAP (UK, Italy, Japan) was started in 2022; according to a Wikipedia page the joint FCAS (NGF) project was terminated in 2026. The game year 2005 does not correspond to reality (see the note below). In the game the node is labelled "low-observable, F-22 class" (in the world this is the 5th generation). The true 6th generation (NGAD, GCAP) is not in service: it is a prospect, not yet represented in the tree.

node-s8-eng-aesa_sensor_fusion =
    .name = AESA radar and sensor fusion
    .short = AESA
    .anchor = APG-77: flight tests (11.1997, estimate)
    .history = The Japanese J/APG-1 radar on the Mitsubishi F-2 (first flight 7 October 1995, in service from 2000) is considered the first combat airborne AESA; the American APG-63(V)2 on 18 F-15Cs entered service in 1999. The F-22 with the APG-77 radar (1,956 transmit/receive modules) entered service on 15 December 2005. The F-35 develops sensor data fusion (APG-81, EW, DAS), and the fusion software delayed the programme. [Fact]

node-s5-cmp-ceramic_matrix_composite =
    .name = Ceramic matrix composite
    .short = CMC
    .anchor = GE: CMC shrouds in LEAP (2016, estimate)
    .history = The LEAP in 2016 became the first widely used product with a ceramic composite: a CMC high-pressure turbine shroud works up to about 1,300 °C (2,400 °F), and the material withstands 300-400 °F more than metals. It entered service on 2 August 2016 on the A320neo. The GE9X (first flight 13 March 2018, FAA certificate 25 September 2020) has 65 CMC parts, all static, and the first-stage blades remain metal. [Fact]

node-s5-prc-cmc_fabrication =
    .name = CMC fabrication
    .short = CMC fab.
    .anchor = estimate 2016
    .history = [Fact] General Electric's ceramic composites (SiC fibres with a coating containing boron nitride, in an SiC matrix) have been developed for more than 30 years since the 1980s (Niskayuna, New York), with support from the Department of Energy, the Department of Defense and NASA. The LEAP high-pressure turbine shrouds of CMC were certified by the FAA and EASA in May 2016; the LEAP became the first widely used product with CMC, with an operating temperature up to 2,400 °F. Five CMC parts are planned for the GE9X (in service on the 777X around 2020). Corrected: the earlier "GE9X combustor in 2018+" was replaced with the data of the page.

node-s8-eng-adaptive_cycle_engine =
    .name = Adaptive cycle engine
    .short = Adaptive
    .anchor = GE XA100: tests (2021, estimate)
    .history = AFRL announced the ADVENT programme on 26 March 2007; it was followed by AETD (2012) and the Adaptive Engine Transition Program (AETP, 2016). The GE XA100 began testing in December 2020, the second unit on 26 August 2021, and on 25 March 2022 it was first run in an Air Force test stand; +10 % thrust and +25 % efficiency are claimed. The Pratt & Whitney XA101 was first run in 2021, but in 2023 the Air Force chose an upgrade of the F135 core, not an adaptive engine, for the F-35. There are no production adaptive engines in service. [Fact]

node-s8-prn-phased_array_radar_pesa =
    .name = Passive phased-array radar
    .short = PESA
    .anchor = MiG-31, Zaslon radar: first flight (1975)
    .history = [Fact] The Zaslon radar (BRLS-8B) of the MiG-31 is called the first production airborne passive phased array on a fighter; developed in 1975-1980 at the Tikhomirov institute, accepted in 1981. [Assumption] All this information comes from secondary sources; no primary ones (NIIP, archives) were found in the search. [Not confirmed] The ground phased arrays of the 1960s (AN/FPS-85) and the advantage over a mechanical antenna are not confirmed by pages.

node-s5-prn-gaas_wafer_growth =
    .name = Gallium arsenide crystal growth
    .short = GaAs growth
    .anchor = Mullin et al.: GaAs bulk growth, LEC (1965)
    .history = [Not confirmed] The statement comes from a search snippet; the opened NTRS record 19870013853 turned out to be a different document (a JPL patent "Total immersion crystal growth", Morrison, 1987) and does not contain it. The snippet: one NASA report attributes the first demonstration of the liquid-encapsulated growth method (LEC) to a paper by Metz and co-authors in J. Appl. Phys. 33 (1962) and the first growth of GaAs and InAs crystals to Mullin and colleagues. [Assumption] The report text is garbled by recognition; the exact dates of GaAs growth and the role of Plessey/RRE are not confirmed. [Not confirmed] The use of substrates in phased-array microwave modules is not shown by pages; Mullin's 2015 review describes the history of LEC.

node-s5-cmp-gaas_mmic =
    .name = GaAs monolithic microwave circuits
    .short = GaAs MMIC
    .anchor = Plessey: first GaAs MMIC (1976)
    .history = Monolithic microwave integrated circuits were first made on gallium arsenide with MESFET field-effect transistors, later moving to HEMTs and heterojunction bipolar transistors. The DARPA MIMIC programme funded GaAs foundries for transmit/receive modules; its years are given differently in the source (1987-1995 and 1988-1999). The year of the first MMIC (game date 1976) is not confirmed. [Fact in part; the date [not confirmed]]

node-s8-cmp-solid_state_tr_module =
    .name = Solid-state transmit/receive module
    .short = T/R module
    .anchor = DARPA MIMIC: hardware phase (1988, estimate)
    .history = The APG-77 radar on the F-22 contains 1,956 solid-state transmit/receive modules; an early scheme used an equal number of separate transmit and receive modules. Whether they replaced travelling-wave tubes is not checked in a separate source. The game date 1988 [is not confirmed]. [Fact in part]

node-s8-prn-programmable_signal_processing =
    .name = Programmable signal processing
    .short = Signal proc
    .anchor = APG-63: programmable processor (1979, estimate)
    .history = [Fact] The AN/UYS-1 Advanced Signal Processor is named as the first programmable signal processor for high-speed modular processing; the IBM contract was won in 1973, limited production from 1979, full production from 1981, about 1,700 units in the fleet. [Fact] It serves to process sonobuoy data and is part of the systems of US Navy anti-submarine platforms, not of radars. [Not confirmed] The use of programmable processors in airborne radars is not shown by pages; in the game the node belongs to 1979, which coincides with the start of UYS-1 production.

node-s8-eng-digital_beamforming =
    .name = Digital beamforming
    .short = Digital beam
    .anchor = Experimental digital arrays (1990, estimate)
    .history = The theory of digital antenna arrays began to be developed in 1962 in the USSR (Vladimir Varyukhin), in 1957 Melton and Bailey worked on analogue signal processing, and from 1960 fast computers were used for direction finding. The first operational digital beamforming system could not be confirmed; the game date 1990 [is not confirmed]. [Fact in part]

node-s8-prn-relaxed_static_stability =
    .name = Relaxed static stability
    .short = Unstable af.
    .anchor = CCV programme start (1972, estimate)
    .history = [Fact] The F-4 CCV (PACT) tested low longitudinal stability and a short forward horizontal canard; the conclusion of a 1974 SAE report: a large gain in manoeuvrability, the predictions confirmed in flight tests. [Fact] The YF-4E with an analogue fly-by-wire system without a mechanical backup flew on 22 January 1973; the programme ended on 23 August 1974. [Fact] The YF-16 used a low-stability layout with digital fly-by-wire. [Not confirmed] The date of the first flight of the F-16 (1974) is not confirmed by the pages of this check.

node-s8-cmp-digital_flight_control_computer =
    .name = Digital flight control computer
    .short = Flight comp.
    .anchor = F-8 DFBW phase II: three AP-101 computers (1976, estimate)
    .history = The F-8 DFBW computer ran on an Apollo Guidance Computer, with the first fully digital flight on 25 May 1972; the aircraft is named as the first digital fly-by-wire aircraft without mechanical backup. For combat aircraft the quadruple-redundant system of the F-16 (1978) matters, and the Airbus A320, which entered service in 1988. The year a digital computer appeared on the F/A-18 is not checked. [Fact in part]

node-s8-prn-electronic_engine_control =
    .name = Electronic engine control
    .short = Eng. control
    .anchor = Olympus 593 (Concorde): first flight (02.03.1969)
    .history = [Fact] The DEEC for the F100-PW-100 passed flight evaluation on the F-15 (a single-channel digital control with a hydromechanical backup, full authority); the tests ran for about a year and a half (papers of 1982 and 1984), and the problems of the first two phases were removed by the fourth. [Fact] The result led to the F100 EMD on the F-15 and to the HIDEC programme (joint engine and aircraft control). [Not confirmed] The exact years of the flight tests, the analogue controls of the 1960s and the node date 1969 are not confirmed by pages (NTRS 19860015877 did not open).

node-s5-cmp-high_modulus_fibre =
    .name = High-modulus fibre
    .short = Fibre
    .anchor = RAE: high-modulus carbon fibre (1963)
    .history = In 1963 W. Watt, L. Phillips and W. Johnson of the Royal Aircraft Establishment at Farnborough developed a process for making high-strength carbon fibre from polyacrylonitrile; in parallel Akio Shindo in Japan obtained PAN fibre in the early 1960s. Rolls-Royce used carbon fibre composite in the RB211 fan: the blade failed in a bird-strike test in May 1970, the firm was insolvent by January 1971 and was nationalised after receivership on 4 February 1971. [Fact]

node-s8-prn-thrust_vectoring_nozzle =
    .name = Thrust-vectoring nozzle
    .short = TV nozzle
    .anchor = F-15 S/MTD: first thrust-vectoring flight (16.05.1989)
    .history = [Fact] The F-15 S/MTD received nozzles with pitch thrust vectoring and forward canards in 1988; the USAF evaluated them in 1989-1991; the first phase of short take-off tests was completed on 31 January 1989. [Fact] According to Flight International, a derivative of the P&W nozzle from this aircraft is fitted to the F119 for the F-22. [Fact] The X-31 first flew on 11 October 1990 as a thrust vectoring research aircraft. [Not confirmed] The entry into service of the F-22 (2005) and the role of the Harrier are not confirmed by pages.

node-s8-prn-rcs_computational_prediction =
    .name = Computational RCS prediction
    .short = RCS calc.
    .anchor = Lockheed: Echo 1 programme (1975)
    .history = [Fact] Echo 1 (1975) made it possible to predict the reflection of a shape; in the same year DARPA accepted an unsolicited proposal from Lockheed. [Fact] The Echo 1 programme (Lockheed, author Denys Overholser by search results; the page does not name the author) estimated the RCS of designs; early tests showed errors in the calculation of edge diffraction, and Ufimtsev's results were put into the programme. [Fact] The calculation was two-dimensional and allowed faceted shapes but not smooth ones. [Not confirmed] The roles of Overholser and Ufimtsev on the opened page, and the later methods of moments (B-2), are not confirmed by pages (the DARPA PDF cannot be read).

node-s8-eng-integrated_avionics_architecture =
    .name = Integrated avionics architecture
    .short = Avionics
    .anchor = Pave Pillar (1986, estimate)
    .history = The avionics of the F-22 was built on the DoD "Pave Pillar" architecture with technologies of the VHSIC programme; two Hughes CIP computers process sensor data, 10.5 billion operations per second each. The MIL-STD-1553B bus standard was issued in 1978 as a single one for three branches of the armed forces. The concept of integrated modular avionics is believed to have arisen in the avionics of fourth-generation fighters. [Fact]

node-s5-cmp-silicon_carbide_fibre =
    .name = Silicon carbide fibre
    .short = SiC fibre
    .anchor = Yajima: SiC fibre from polycarbosilane (1975)
    .history = Professor Seishi Yajima in Japan in 1975 obtained silicon carbide fibre from the polymer polycarbosilane (the Yajima process); US patent 4,052,430 was granted on 4 October 1977. Nippon Carbon developed it into the production fibre Nicalon; the year 1978 is given in a paper by a company engineer, but the context is truncated in the extract. The fibre became the basis of SiC/SiC composites for the hot parts of engines. [Fact; the year serial production began [not confirmed]]

node-s5-prn-matrix_infiltration_cvi =
    .name = Chemical vapour infiltration
    .short = CVI
    .anchor = Naslain: first CMC by CVI (1977)
    .history = [Fact] Oak Ridge National Laboratory developed forced chemical vapour infiltration (FCVI) with temperature and pressure gradients for thick-walled ceramic parts; in 1994 discs of 24.6 cm diameter and 1.27 cm thickness were obtained. [Fact] A 1987 paper by D. Stinton described silicon carbide composites with continuous fibres produced by CVI, and infiltration models. [Not confirmed] The start of development in the 1960s-70s (Oak Ridge, SEP) and the use of CMC in engines in the 2010s are not confirmed by pages.

node-s5-prn-environmental_barrier_coating =
    .name = Environmental barrier coating
    .short = EBC
    .anchor = NASA/GE/P&W: first-generation coating (2000, estimate)
    .history = [Fact] NASA Glenn describes the evolution of EBC systems for SiC/SiC through several generations (Zhu, 2016); a separate 2017 presentation (Lee et al., Santa Fe) is devoted to current tests. [Not confirmed by opened pages] The following is taken from search snippets: the coating was developed by NASA Glenn with GE and P&W in the 1990s, building on work on mullite coatings on SiC. [Fact] The first generation EPM Gen I (BSAS/mullite+BSAS/Si, 1995-2000), the second UEET Gen II (RE2Si2O7 or RE2SiO5, 2000-2004); the next direction for 2020-2025 is CMC at 2,400 °F and EBC at 2,700 °F. [Fact] The tests ran on the NASA high-pressure burner rig. [Not confirmed] Serial use in the 2010s and the temperature of 1,300 °C are not confirmed by pages.

node-s8-prn-variable_cycle_engine =
    .name = Variable cycle engine
    .short = Var. cycle
    .anchor = YF120 on YF-22: first flight (29.09.1990)
    .history = [Fact] The US Air Force chose the P&W F119 over the GE YF120 for the ATF in August 1991; according to a Defense ARJ article, the YF120 was superior to the YF119 at high Mach numbers but was considered riskier, and P&W had accumulated more test hours. [Fact] The YF120 changed its bypass ratio for different flight modes. [Not confirmed] The predecessors GE YJ101/GE21 in the 1970s and the ADVENT/AETD programmes by year are not confirmed by pages (the Air Force museum page did not open; ADVENT from 2007 is confirmed on the GE page at the node adaptive_third_stream_fan).

node-s8-prn-adaptive_third_stream_fan =
    .name = Adaptive third-stream fan
    .short = 3rd stream
    .anchor = GE ADVENT: engine run (26.11.2013)
    .history = [Fact] GE and the US Air Force have been working on an adaptive engine since 2007: first ADVENT, then AETD. [Fact] The second XA100 engine was run on 26 August 2021 in Evendale, the last prototype under AETP; the first engine had been tested since December 2020. [Not confirmed by an opened page] 22 December, phase 2 at AEDC and November 2023: GE began testing the first XA100 engine in Evendale on 22 December 2020; the second engine: phase 1 from 26 August 2021, phase 2 at the Arnold stand (AEDC) on 25 March (per a GE press release) with completion of the main milestone of the AETP programme. [Fact] GE describes the third stream as a means of thermal management; in November 2023 a third cycle of tests was reported. [Not confirmed] The role of the Pratt & Whitney XA101, the ADVENT/AETD programmes and the node date 2013 are not confirmed by pages; no production installation was confirmed at the time of the check.

